Using XGBoost in Pipelines 
Extreme Gradient Boosting with XGBoost course! In this final chapter, you'll learn how to integrate XGBoost estimators into scikit-learn pipelines, handle categorical features and missing values using data transformers like DictVectorizer and Imputer, and build end-to-end machine learning workflows.

📌 Key Topics & Concepts Covered
1. Scikit-learn Pipelines Review
Pipelines take a list of named tuples (steps) containing transformers and a final estimator.

They allow sequential execution of data transformations followed by model training, maintaining a clean and leak-free workflow.

Pipelines integrate seamlessly with cross_val_score, GridSearchCV, and RandomizedSearchCV.

2. Handling Categorical Data
LabelEncoder vs. OneHotEncoder: Traditional two-step encoding methods can be cumbersome and aren't fully pipeline-friendly for multi-column categorical datasets.

DictVectorizer: Converts a list of feature mappings (dictionaries) directly into numerical vectors, making it ideal for encoding categorical columns inside a scikit-learn pipeline in a single step.

3. Handling Missing Data
Real-world datasets often contain missing values (NaN).

Using scikit-learn's Imputer (or SimpleImputer) inside a pipeline allows you to impute missing values (using mean, median, or most frequent strategies) before passing the data to your XGBoost model.

XGBoost also handles missing values natively by learning the optimal default direction for missing splits during tree construction.

